IMPORT prelude

-- Singapore University of Technology and Design Act 2011 (Singapore) -- what the university company may award (s 3), which of its
-- acts need the Minister's prior written consent and what happens to an act done without it (s 7), how Parliament's money may be spent
-- (s 8(2)), the Minister's power to demand information from ANY person and the offence of refusing or obstructing (s 9(2), (4)),
-- student bodies and the Societies Act 1966 (s 10), and the Act prevailing over the company's own documents (s 11(1)).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/SUTDA2011.txt. The deposit says it "incorporates all
-- amendments up to and including 1 December 2021 and comes into operation on 31 December 2021"; the only amendment it annotates is
-- [40/2018], at s 7(2). The Act's commencement date is given as [18 October 2011].
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Requirement REQ-0217 in subjects/sg/requirements.jsonl: tier 3 of the remaining Singapore Acts, ordered by
-- everyday-life relevance. The requirement asks what the Act decides for a person or business it applies to; no scenario has asked a
-- sharper question yet. The Act is eleven sections long and mostly governs the university company itself; what reaches an outsider
-- is s 7(3) (an act or agreement made without the Minister's consent has no effect), s 9 (the information demand and the $1,000
-- offence), s 10 (student bodies) and s 3 (degrees). SCOPED to those, with s 8(2) and s 11(1).
-- Not encoded: the accountability framework, quality assurance and external review (s 4), the Minister's policy directions (s 5), the
-- appointment and removal of trustees (s 6) beyond its interaction with s 7(1)(e), the duty to fund (s 8(1)), access to records and
-- the public summary of financial statements (s 9(1), (3)), and s 11(2) (other written law still applies).
--
-- The ARRANGEMENT OF SECTIONS at the top of the deposit is one line out of step with the body (it lists "1." with no heading and
-- numbers "Short title" as 2); the section numbers here follow the body.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text, following the conventions of the finished naive rows (the writing-l4-rules skill
-- was not available in this session). No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Singapore University of Technology and Design Act 2011`

§§ `3 -- what the university company may award`

-- s 3: "The function of the university company is to pursue, within the limits of the financial resources available to it, the
-- objects provided by its constituent documents and, in particular, to confer and award degrees, diplomas and certificates, including
-- honorary degrees and other distinctions."
DECLARE Award IS ONE OF
    `a degree`
    `a diploma`
    `a certificate`
    `an honorary degree`
    `another distinction`
    `a professional licence to practise`

-- A licence to practise is not among the awards s 3 names; it is included as a contrast. Whether the constituent documents' objects
-- could reach it was not read (they are not deposited).
GIVEN `the award` IS A Award
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 3 names the award as a function` IF
    CONSIDER `the award`
    WHEN `a professional licence to practise` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `7 -- the Minister's prior written consent`

-- s 7(1): "The prior written consent of the Minister is required for -- (a) the admission of any person as a member of the university
-- company and the removal of any such member; (b) the disposal of the whole or substantially the whole of the university company's
-- undertaking or property; (c) the voluntary winding up of the university company; (d) the addition, deletion or alteration of any
-- provision of the constituent documents of the university company; and (e) the removal of any trustee from the Board."
-- (2): these apply "in addition to" the Companies Act 1967 and the Insolvency, Restructuring and Dissolution Act 2018.
-- (3): "Any act done or agreement made in contravention of subsection (1) has no effect and is unenforceable at law."
DECLARE Matter IS ONE OF
    `admitting a member of the company`
    `removing a member of the company`
    `disposing of the whole undertaking or property`
    `disposing of substantially the whole undertaking or property`
    `disposing of part of the property`
    `a voluntary winding up`
    `a winding up by the court`
    `altering the constituent documents`
    `removing a trustee`
    `appointing a trustee`
    `an ordinary supply contract`

-- A winding up by the court is included as a contrast: s 7(1)(c) names only the VOLUNTARY winding up.
GIVEN `the matter` IS A Matter
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Minister's prior written consent is required` IF
    CONSIDER `the matter`
    WHEN `admitting a member of the company` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `removing a member of the company` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `disposing of the whole undertaking or property` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `disposing of substantially the whole undertaking or property` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a voluntary winding up` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `altering the constituent documents` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `removing a trustee` THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- Consent must be BOTH prior and written. Consent given afterwards, or orally, does not satisfy s 7(1); the text gives no way to
-- ratify. Encoded only as what s 7(3) says: the act or agreement "has no effect and is unenforceable at law". Compliance with the
-- Companies Act 1967 and IRDA 2018 (s 7(2)) is not modelled; meeting s 7 does not by itself make the act effective under them.
GIVEN `the matter` IS A Matter, `consent was given` IS A BOOLEAN, `consent came before the act` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `consent was in writing` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 7(3) makes the act of no effect and unenforceable` IF
        `the Minister's prior written consent is required` `the matter`
    AND (NOT (`consent was given` AND `consent came before the act` AND `consent was in writing`))

§§ `8(2) -- spending Parliament's money`

-- s 8(2): "All moneys paid to the university company under subsection (1) may only be applied or expended by the university company
-- for such objects provided by its constituent documents as the Minister may allow." Moneys from other sources are not restricted
-- by s 8(2).
GIVEN `the money came from Parliament under s 8(1)` IS A BOOLEAN, `the object is in the constituent documents` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the Minister allows the object` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 8(2) permits the spending` IF
        (NOT `the money came from Parliament under s 8(1)`)
    OR  (`the object is in the constituent documents` AND `the Minister allows the object`)

§§ `9(2), (4) -- information demands and the offence`

-- s 9(2): the Minister or an authorised person "may require any person to provide him or her with any information in that person's
-- possession, or to which that person has access, that the Minister or authorised person considers necessary for ascertaining -- (a)
-- whether moneys paid to the university company under section 8 were applied or expended in accordance with that section; and (b) any
-- other matters that the Minister or authorised person considers necessary."
--
-- Limb (b) reaches "any other matters" the Minister considers necessary, so the only real limit on a demand is the Minister's own view
-- of necessity. Encoded as such: the s 8 purpose makes no difference to whether the demand is within s 9(2).
GIVEN `the Minister considers the information necessary` IS A BOOLEAN, `the purpose is checking the s 8 money` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the purpose is another matter the Minister considers necessary` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the demand is within s 9(2)` IF
        `the Minister considers the information necessary`
    AND (`the purpose is checking the s 8 money` OR `the purpose is another matter the Minister considers necessary`)

-- s 9(4): "Any person who, without reasonable excuse, fails to comply with any requirement of the Minister or an authorised person under
-- subsection (2), or who otherwise hinders, obstructs or delays the Minister or an authorised person in the performance of his or her
-- duties or in the exercise of his or her powers under this section, shall be guilty of an offence and shall be liable on conviction
-- to a fine not exceeding $1,000."
--
-- READING: "without reasonable excuse" is placed in the first limb only, so it is encoded as qualifying failure to comply and not
-- hindering, obstructing or delaying. A court might read it across both limbs; the text does not settle it.
GIVEN `a requirement was made of the person under s 9(2)` IS A BOOLEAN, `the person complied` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the person had a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN, `the person hindered, obstructed or delayed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `an offence under s 9(4)` IF
        (`a requirement was made of the person under s 9(2)` AND (NOT `the person complied`) AND (NOT `the person had a reasonable excuse`))
    OR  `the person hindered, obstructed or delayed`

-- The penalty is a fine only: no term of imprisonment is provided.
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `s 9(4) maximum fine` IS 1000

GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 9(4) provides imprisonment` IS FALSE

GIVEN `the fine` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the fine is within s 9(4)` IF
    `the fine` AT LEAST 0 AND `the fine` AT MOST `s 9(4) maximum fine`

§§ `10 -- student bodies and the Societies Act 1966`

-- s 10(1): "Despite anything to the contrary in the Societies Act 1966, the provisions of that Act have effect in relation to any student
-- body constituted pursuant to the provisions of the constituent documents of the university company."
-- (2): the Minister charged with the responsibility for societies "may, by order in the Gazette, exempt any student body mentioned in
-- subsection (1) from all or any of the provisions of the Societies Act 1966 subject to any conditions specified in the order."
--
-- INFERENCE: the words "despite anything to the contrary" suggest the Societies Act would otherwise exclude such a body; that Act was
-- not read. A student club NOT constituted under the constituent documents is outside s 10 and is left to the Societies Act itself,
-- which this row does not encode. READING: an exemption is treated as removing the provision only while its conditions are met.
GIVEN `constituted under the constituent documents` IS A BOOLEAN, `exempted from the provision by Gazette order` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `the order's conditions are met` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 10 applies the Societies Act provision to the student body` IF
        `constituted under the constituent documents`
    AND (NOT (`exempted from the provision by Gazette order` AND `the order's conditions are met`))

§§ `11(1) -- the Act prevails over the company's documents`

-- s 11(1): "Any provision of the constituent documents, or any regulation of the university company made pursuant to any provision of
-- the constituent documents, that is inconsistent with any provision of this Act is, to the extent of the inconsistency, void."
DECLARE Instrument IS ONE OF
    `a provision of the memorandum or articles`
    `a regulation made pursuant to the constituent documents`
    `a contract with a third party`

-- A contract is not an instrument s 11(1) reaches; a contract made in breach of s 7(1) is dealt with by s 7(3) instead.
GIVEN `the instrument` IS A Instrument, `it is inconsistent with the Act` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `s 11(1) makes it void to the extent of the inconsistency` IF
        `it is inconsistent with the Act`
    AND CONSIDER `the instrument`
        WHEN `a contract with a third party` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE
